.TH CLOUDFS 4
.SH NAME
cloudfs \- serve a cloud provider's metadata service as a file tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount {cloudfs
[
.BI -a " baseurl"
]
[
.BI -H " 'name: value'"
]
.BI } " dir"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cloudfs
serves 9P on its standard input, presenting a flat directory of
read-only files, each of which is one entry of the instance metadata
service.  Reading a file makes the HTTP request and returns what the
service said.
.PP
The files are
.BR id ,
.BR name ,
.BR hostname ,
.BR zone ,
.BR machine-type ,
.BR project ,
.B ip
and
.BR token .
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BI -a " baseurl"
The metadata service's base URL.  The default is
.B http://169.254.169.254/
which is where both Compute Engine and EC2 answer.
.TP
.BI -H " 'name: value'"
The header the service demands.  The default is
.BR "Metadata-Flavor: Google" .
.PP
Every read is a fresh request, made when the file is opened.  The
token this instance is issued expires, so a cached one is a bug that
waits an hour to appear.
.PP
The point is that the provider's control plane becomes part of the name
space rather than a library to link against.
.B Cat
.B /cloud/token
replaces an SDK, and nothing in the program that consumes it knows
which provider it is running on.  See
.B doc/inferno-cloud-modern.ms
for what this is the first piece of.
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
.EX
cloudinit
mount {cloudfs} /cloud
cat /cloud/zone
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/cloudfs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cloudinit (1),
.IR hget (1),
.IR httpclient (2)
.SH BUGS
The file names and the paths behind them are Compute Engine's.  Another
provider is another table in the source and the same program, but that
table is not written yet.
.PP
A read fails, rather than returning nothing, when the service cannot be
reached.  On Compute Engine that is what happens without the host route
.IR cloudinit (1)
installs.
